  <text>
      <body>
         
         <!-- LEAD SECTION -->
         <div type="section" n="lead">
            <head>Lead Section</head>
            <p>
               The history of <orgName ref="#org_Nintendo">Nintendo</orgName>, a Japanese video game company based in <placeName ref="#place_Kyoto">Kyoto</placeName>, starts in <date when="1889">1889</date> when <persName ref="#pers_FusajiroYamauchi">Fusajiro Yamauchi</persName> founded <quote>"Yamauchi Nintendo"</quote>, a producer of <rs type="object" ref="#item_Hanafuda">hanafuda playing cards</rs>. His successor <persName ref="#pers_HiroshiYamauchi">Hiroshi Yamauchi</persName> had <orgName ref="#org_Nintendo">Nintendo</orgName> producing toys and video games.
            </p>
            <p>
               <persName ref="#pers_ShigeruMiyamoto">Shigeru Miyamoto</persName> designed <title ref="#work_DonkeyKong">Donkey Kong</title> (<date when="1981">1981</date>) for <rs type="object" ref="#item_ArcadeCabinet">arcades</rs>: <orgName ref="#org_Nintendo">Nintendo</orgName>'s first international hit game, and origin of the company's mascot, <rs type="character" ref="#char_Jumpman">Mario</rs>. After the American video game crash of <date when="1983">1983</date>, <orgName ref="#org_Nintendo">Nintendo</orgName> released their Japanese Famicom home console as the <rs type="object" ref="#item_NES">Nintendo Entertainment System (NES)</rs> in <date when="1985">1985</date>. <persName ref="#pers_ShigeruMiyamoto">Miyamoto</persName>'s innovative titles, <title ref="#work_MarioBros">Super Mario Bros.</title> and <title ref="#work_TheLegendOfZelda">The Legend of Zelda</title> (<date when="1987">1987</date>), greatly influenced gaming. The <rs type="object" ref="#item_GameBoy">Game Boy</rs> handheld console (<date when="1989">1989</date>) was extraordinarily successful, alongside the <title ref="#work_Pokemon">Pokémon</title> media franchise.
            </p>
            <p>
               In <date when="2002">2002</date>, <persName ref="#pers_SatoruIwata">Satoru Iwata</persName> became president, leading development of the <rs type="object" ref="#item_NintendoDS">Nintendo DS</rs> handheld (<date when="2004">2004</date>) and the <rs type="object" ref="#item_Wii">Wii</rs> home console (<date when="2006">2006</date>). Before dying in <date when="2015">2015</date>, <persName ref="#pers_SatoruIwata">Iwata</persName> led development of the successful <rs type="object" ref="#item_NintendoSwitch">Nintendo Switch</rs> (<date when="2017">2017</date>). Today, Nintendo's historical heritage is preserved at the <placeName ref="#place_NintendoMuseum">Nintendo Museum</placeName> (opened in <date when="2024">2024</date>) in <placeName ref="#place_Kyoto">Kyoto</placeName>, dedicated to <term ref="#concept_VideoGamePreservation">video game preservation</term>.
            </p>
         </div>

         <!-- SECTION: 1889–1949: HANAFUDA CARDS -->
         <div type="section" n="1889-1949">
            <head>1889–1949: Hanafuda cards</head>
            <p>
               <orgName ref="#org_Nintendo">Nintendo</orgName> was founded as Yamauchi Nintendo by <persName ref="#pers_FusajiroYamauchi">Fusajiro Yamauchi</persName> on September 23, <date when="1889">1889</date>. Based in <placeName ref="#place_Kyoto">Kyoto, Japan</placeName>, the business produced and marketed <rs type="object" ref="#item_Hanafuda">hanafuda</rs>, a type of Japanese playing card. During the era of <date from="1889" to="1929">1889–1929</date>, the company expanded card production. In 1929, <persName ref="#pers_FusajiroYamauchi">Fusajiro Yamauchi</persName> retired and allowed his adopted son-in-law to take over.
            </p>
            <p>
               In the period of <date from="1949" to="2002">1949–2002</date>, under <persName ref="#pers_HiroshiYamauchi">Hiroshi Yamauchi</persName>, <orgName ref="#org_Nintendo">Nintendo</orgName> transformed from a playing card manufacturer into a global video game giant, before <persName ref="#pers_SatoruIwata">Satoru Iwata</persName> took leadership in <date from="2002" to="2015">2002–2015</date>.
            </p>
         </div>

      </body>
  </text>
